1
00:00:00,233 --> 00:00:02,333
Seawater soaks up radio — so
how does

2
00:00:02,333 --> 00:00:04,167
a hidden submarine get a
message?

3
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:08,400
The higher the frequency,
the faster it disappears.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,633
Very-low-frequency radio
gets in

5
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:14,067
but, according to the US
Navy, only tens of feet.

6
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:17,867
So to listen, a submarine
has to get an antenna up

7
00:00:17,867 --> 00:00:21,100
near the surface — which
limits how deep and fast it

8
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:23,333
can go, and makes it easier
to spot.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,233
The US also ran an
extremely-low-frequency

10
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:31,067
system, at about 76 hertz,
that reached submarines

11
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:34,200
hundreds of feet down — but
only one way,

12
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,733
slowly. It was shut down in
2004.

13
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:40,533
So every message to a hidden
submarine is a trade:

14
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:43,067
to hear it, the boat gives
up some depth,

15
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:44,133
speed or stealth.
